summary: cargo build for one crate's work, scripts/complete-build.sh for everything — the frontend bundle, the rendered documentation, the installed configuration — and what each needs.

!! The quick build

```
cargo build                      # every crate, debug
cargo build -p metafolder-daemon # one crate
cargo run -p metafolder-daemon   # the daemon, port 7523
cargo run -p metafolder-cli -- --help    # mf
cargo run -p metafolder-gui      # the GUI (build the frontend first)
```

This is enough for work inside one Rust crate. It does ''not'' build the GUI's
frontend, render the documentation or install the configuration — and the daemon
and the GUI read their configuration from `~/.config/metafolder/`, with no built-in
copy to fall back on ([[No runtime fallback]]).

The compiler is pinned by `rust-toolchain.toml` (version, plus `clippy`, `rustfmt`
and `llvm-tools`): `rustup` installs it on first use. Bump it there, deliberately
([[Keeping the target directory small]] says why it is pinned).

!! The complete build

```
scripts/complete-build.sh
```

It takes no argument and does, in the one order that works:

# `cargo build`, `cargo test --workspace --no-run`, and `cargo build -p metafolder-core --features sync-config` — the three sets of artifacts the project uses;
# `scripts/prune-target.sh`, which deletes what those builds superseded;
# `npm install`, the first time, then `npm --prefix crates/gui/frontend run build` — the Svelte shell, which Tauri embeds into the GUI binary at compile time;
# `scripts/doc build` — this wiki, rendered into `docs/wiki/dist/`;
# `target/debug/metafolder-sync-config` — the shipped defaults, the shipped scripts and the rendered documentation applied to the configuration repository ([[Updating the configuration]], [[Shipped defaults in the source tree]]).

Use it after pulling, after touching the frontend or a shipped default, and whenever
`target/` has grown. `scripts/test-complete-build.sh` holds its order and its paths.

!! What needs rebuilding after an edit

|!Edited |!Then |
|a panel type under `crates/gui/default-config/panel-types/` |`metafolder-sync-config` (the panels are served from the configuration repository), and restart the GUI |
|`crates/gui/panel-shim/*.js` |`cargo build -p metafolder-gui` — the helpers are compiled into the binary — and restart |
|the Svelte shell, `crates/gui/frontend/src/` |`npm --prefix crates/gui/frontend run build`, then `cargo build -p metafolder-gui`, and restart |
|a note of this wiki |`scripts/doc build`, then `metafolder-sync-config` |
|a default configuration file, a shipped script |`metafolder-sync-config` |

A plain `cargo build` succeeds without a built frontend — `build.rs` writes a
placeholder — but the window is then empty.

`metafolder-sync-config` is behind a Cargo feature, so a plain `cargo build` does not
build it. Always scope the feature to its crate (`-p metafolder-core --features
sync-config`): on the whole workspace it would recompile every crate against a
feature-enabled `core`.

!! What the build needs

* Rust, through `rustup`; `npm` and `node` for the frontend and the wiki.
* The Tauri system libraries to build the `gui` crate (on Arch: `webkit2gtk-4.1 gtk3 librsvg`).
* Nothing else to build or to test. What the GUI needs to ''run'' — `bwrap`, an emoji font, `ffmpeg`, `poppler`, the GStreamer plugins — is in [[GUI runtime dependencies]], checked by `scripts/check-deps.sh`; in a container that forbids WebKit's sandbox, see [[Running inside a container]].
* Optional tools, each skipped out loud by `scripts/check.sh` when missing: `cargo-deny`, `semgrep`, `cargo-llvm-cov` ([[Static checks]]).

!! Disk space

`target/` is never cleaned by cargo. `scripts/prune-target.sh [--dry-run]` removes
the artifacts that newer ones superseded; run it only right after a complete build,
which is what `complete-build.sh` does ([[Keeping the target directory small]]).
`scripts/check.sh` warns when less than 10 GiB is free on the disk holding `target/`
(`METAFOLDER_CHECK_MIN_FREE_GIB`).
